    "An O(n^2) DP works: best[j] = 1 + max(best[i]) over earlier i with nums[i] < nums[j]. Can you avoid scanning every earlier i?",
    "Keep tails[k] = the smallest possible last value of a rising pick of length k + 1 seen so far. This array is always strictly increasing.",
    "For each x, binary-search the first tail >= x and overwrite it with x (or append x if every tail is smaller). The answer is the length of tails."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function longestRise(nums) {\n  const tails = []\n  for (const x of nums) {\n    let lo = 0\n    let hi = tails.length\n    while (lo < hi) {\n      const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1\n      if (tails[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1\n      else hi = mid\n    }\n    tails[lo] = x\n  }\n  return tails.length\n}\n",
    "py": "from bisect import bisect_left\n\n\ndef longest_rise(nums):\n    tails = []\n    for x in nums:\n        i = bisect_left(tails, x)\n        if i == len(tails):\n            tails.append(x)\n        else:\n            tails[i] = x\n    return len(tails)\n",
    "explanation": "tails[k] stores the smallest value that can end a strictly rising pick of length k + 1. Replacing the first tail that is >= x keeps every tail as small as possible, and appending happens exactly when x extends the longest pick so far. Because tails stays sorted, each step is a binary search; tails itself is not necessarily a valid subsequence, only its length is meaningful."
  },
